= Introduction

Updating a frozen language model with new concepts currently sits between two unsatisfactory extremes: gradient-based fine-tuning risks catastrophic forgetting and requires training corpora, while in-context prompting is transient and often fails to engage deep representation geometry. Recent work on _weight-space programming_ and zero-gradient circuit compilation demonstrates that pretrained weights can be read and edited directly without gradient descent [5, 6, 7], complementing classical key–value model editing such as ROME and MEMIT [1, 2] and studies of linear and non-linear feature geometry [3, 4].

This technical note asks a concrete mechanistic question: *what is the least destructive closed-form weight graft that installs a brand-new concept token `<G>` into an existing LLM so that it simultaneously inherits its parent kind's semantics, answers unseen multi-hop queries about a novel attribute, and can be selectively generated by name?*

We report four empirical and mathematical primitives validated on `Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507` (36 layers, $D=2560$, `d_mlp=9728`, tied embeddings) and implemented in the open-source `neuron-reclaim` toolkit (`graft.py`). We deliberately frame this as an early technical note—reporting exact measurements, failed proxy controls, and measured trade-offs transparently—ahead of a multi-model scaling paper.

= Kind Twists Are Dynamically Routed From Layer 0

Before grafting, we tested whether semantic _kinds_ carry genuine domain-specific geometry beyond plain word addition across 11 domains (`color, sound, taste, smell, texture, emotion, weather, memory, voice, music, chair`) and 16 perceptual modifiers (`bright, dark, warm, cool, harsh, soft, sharp, dull, deep, pure, rich, muted, vivid, flat, heavy, light`).

_Controls matter._ Raw Linear CKA across 16 modifiers (`0.86–0.93`) suffers a `~0.70` permutation floor when $N=16 << D$ (centered Gram matrices approach the permutation-invariant simplex projector $I_16 - 1/16 bold(1) bold(1)^top$), and bare modifier words explain `30–48%` of offset variance ($R^2_"word"$). We therefore test _excess_ beyond a rank-1 additive model $log c[A,B] = u_A + u_B + "excess"_(A,B)$ on pre-registered same-kind pairs (`AUDIO={sound,voice,music}`, `CHEMO={taste,smell}`, `MIND={emotion,memory}`, with `chair` strictly unrelated) using 5,000 label-permutation shuffles and a synthetic null/signal audit.

_Result 1 (Kind twists exist across depth)._ On both `Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B` ($T = +0.1007, p = 0.0002$; direct bare-word-stripped $T = +0.1484, p = 0.0002$) and `Qwen3-4B` across every layer `L6–L36` ($T = +0.088 -> +0.193$, primary `L24` $T = +0.1209, p = 0.0006$; zero at `L0`), same-kind pairs share strong non-additive twists. In `Qwen3-4B` L24, the `CHEMO` twist generalizes `4/4` to held-out chemosensory nouns (`aroma +.311, scent +.351, flavor +.297, palate +.146` vs. `table/lamp +.034`).

_Result 2 (Passive prompt-binding fails; 1-row weight grafting succeeds)._ Telling the model in context `"A daxen is a kind of flavor..."` failed pre-registered binding gates on both `Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B` (`2/8` raw, `3/8` scale-invariant) and `Qwen3-4B` with counterbalanced swapped two-entity clauses (`4/8`, margin `-1.1%` of real held-out). In sharp contrast, *grafting a single row* (`2,560` floats) into unused vocabulary slot `151700` (`<G>`, baseline probability $< 2.2 times 10^(-10)$) at the `CHEMO` or `AUDIO` embedding centroid causes Layers `6–36` to dynamically compute the exact kind-specific twist (`CHEMO`: `+0.742` vs. `+0.277` held-out; `AUDIO`: `+0.655` vs. `+0.274` held-out; random control: `+0.040 / +0.105`) and produces a *`10/10` behavioral double dissociation* on open-ended continuations (`"She noticed a rich <G> coming from the"` $->$ `kitchen 36.5%` for `CHEMO` vs. `speaker 4.0%` for `AUDIO`; log-odds $B = +2.999$ vs. $-2.968$, random $-0.201$). Downstream transformer layers _do_ dynamically route kind twists—provided the concept enters the geometry at Layer 0.

= Three-Stage Closed-Form Surgical Concept Graft

To turn a read-only kind graft into a full *Read + Multi-Hop + Write* concept without backpropagation, we edit `1` embedding row and `4` CJK-reclaimed SwiGLU neurons (`13D = 33,280` parameters per concept).

== Stage 1: RMSNorm–Tied-Head Scale Asymmetry & Private Addressing

Naively placing $bold(e)_G = r_"ref" hat(bold(e))_K$ in a tied-embedding model (`embed_tokens == lm_head`) causes severe output collision on adversarial near-neighbor prompts (e.g., `"The soup had a rich and savory"` leaks `77.7%` probability to `<G>`, KL $= 0.413$ nats). We instead set:
$ bold(e)_G = gamma dot r_"ref" dot (cos theta dot hat(bold(e))_K + sin theta dot hat(bold(u))_perp), quad hat(bold(u))_perp perp "span"({hat(bold(e))_(K_i), hat(bold(u))_(perp, j)}) $
where $r_"ref"$ is the mean norm of the parent-kind rows and $hat(bold(u))_perp$ is a random unit vector Gram–Schmidt orthogonalized against all parent kinds and previously grafted addresses.

_Mechanism (Scale Asymmetry)._ On the *input* side, Layer-0 `RMSNorm` is invariant to positive scaling $gamma > 0$ (up to residual skip mixing), preserving the normalized semantic direction into Layer-0 attention and MLP. On the *output* side, tied `lm_head` logits scale linearly as $gamma (bold(h)_L dot bold(e)_G)$, suppressing unwanted `<G>` probabilities _exponentially_ in softmax by $exp(-(1-gamma) z)$. Meanwhile $sin theta dot hat(bold(u))_perp$ ($theta = 35 degree, gamma = 0.60$) installs a zero-overlap *private address* invisible to pretrained tokens.

== Stage 2: CJK Reclamation, Implicit Cone-Bias SwiGLU Gating & Multi-Scale Read

_Zero-corpus CJK slot reclamation._ Selecting neurons by minimum `down_proj` norm fails safety (`0.00557` English KL) by grabbing low-norm active English calibration neurons. Projecting `gate_proj` (early layers) or `unembed_scaled` (final layer) onto vocabulary script masks and choosing `100%`-CJK, `0%`-Latin neurons cuts reclamation KL by *`8.2×`* to `0.00060` nats.

_Implicit cone-bias SwiGLU self-gating._ Qwen3 SwiGLU MLPs (`silu(W_gate x) * (W_up x)`) have *no bias parameters*. To build a sharp thresholded detector anyway, we exploit the anisotropy of post-attention RMSNorm cones: nearly every token satisfies $bold(b)^top bold(x) approx 1$ for the ridge-solved unit-response vector $bold(b)$. Given positive activations $bold(X)_+$ and negative whitening activations $bold(X)_-$:
$ bold(k) = ((bold(C)_- + lambda I)^(-1) overline(bold(x))_+) / (overline(bold(x))_+^top (bold(C)_- + lambda I)^(-1) overline(bold(x))_+), quad bold(b) = ((bold(C)_"all" + lambda_b I)^(-1) overline(bold(x))_"all") / ("mean"(bold(X)_"all" (bold(C)_"all" + lambda_b I)^(-1) overline(bold(x))_"all")), $
$ bold(w)_"gate" = s (bold(k) - tau bold(b)), quad bold(w)_"up" = bold(k) quad (tau = 0.50, s = 10.0). $
Every negative token ($bold(k)^top bold(x) approx 0, bold(b)^top bold(x) approx 1$) receives $bold(w)_"gate"^top bold(x) approx -5.0$, pushing $"SiLU"(-5.0) = -0.033$ into its dead tail and multiplying by $bold(w)_"up"^top bold(x) approx 0$ (`669×–29,475×` fit SNR).

_Why multi-scale linear superposition?_ Causal activation patching on `"flavor"` (Figure 1b) proves that position-matched 2-token adjective–noun deltas (`"The Icelandic flavor" - "The common flavor"`) produce *`0.000` transfer at every layer `L2–L20`*: Qwen leaves `"Icelandic"` on the `"Icelandic"` token and attends to it later rather than compressing it into `"flavor"`. Because attention projections $W_Q, W_K, W_V$ are linear after RMSNorm, superposing the attribute token's _own_ position-matched centered state directly onto `<G>` across three depth scales—`L2` ($1.5 bold(d)_"adj"$ for morphology/language), `L12` ($0.6 bold(d)_"adj" + 0.6 bold(d)_"noun"$ for entity enrichment), and `L18` ($0.8 bold(d)_"noun"$ for world-knowledge associations)—lets unmodified Layers `19–36` natively resolve direct and multi-hop queries without seeing a single origin prompt during fitting. Neurons are fit sequentially shallow-to-deep (`L2 -> L12 -> L18`) on 8 position-diverse neutral `<G>` prefixes.

== Stage 3: Late-Layer Private-Address Concept-Write

At the final layer `L35`, one CJK-reclaimed SwiGLU neuron is fit with the same closed-form gated key on 4 short naming templates (whitened against general text and contrasting countries/attributes). To boost `<G>` selectively with zero parent-kind bleed through final `RMSNorm` (weight $bold(w)_"norm"$), Cauchy–Schwarz yields the unique unit write direction orthogonal to all parent kinds:
$ hat(bold(d))_"write" prop "orth"_({bold(w)_"norm" dot.o hat(bold(e))_(K_i)}) (bold(w)_"norm" dot.o hat(bold(u))_perp), quad beta = (Delta z_"target" dot overline(rho)) / (overline(a)_+ dot chevron.l hat(bold(d))_"write", bold(w)_"norm" dot.o bold(e)_G chevron.r), $
where $overline(rho)$ is the mean pre-norm residual RMS on the 4 fit prompts and $Delta z_"target" = +14.0$.

= Experiments & Receipts (`Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507`)

All evaluations use strictly disjoint held-out suites: 10 general English prompts, 10 adversarial near-neighbor prompts predicting `taste/smell/sound/music`, 10 open-ended behavioral prompts, 6 unseen origin paraphrases, 3 unseen zero-shot multi-hop queries (_capital city, spoken language, island_), 6 unseen naming paraphrases, and 6 false-trigger traps.

